{"attack": true,"case": "The claim purports to defend Popper's preference for 'better-corroborated' theories, but the mechanism silently substitutes a different criterion: empirical content among unfalsified theories. The mechanism states the preference is 'not based on the probability of truth ... but on the severity of tests passed, which indicates high informational content,' then operationalizes selection as 'choosing the theory with highest empirical content that has not yet been falsified.' This conflates corroboration (a historical measure of how severely a theory has been tested and survived) with empirical content (a logical measure of how much a theory rules out). Two unfalsified theories can have identical empirical content yet differ in corroboration because one has passed more severe tests. By collapsing corroboration into 'informational content + not-yet-falsified,' the mechanism defends only the preference for informative surviving conjectures, not the preference for better-corroborated theories as Popper actually uses the term. Thus the defense is a bait-and-switch: it defends a weaker, content-based rule while claiming to defend the corroboration-based rule that is the target of the induction objection. The countercondition about Tichý/Miller further exposes the gap—if 'best-tested' cannot be coherently defined as 'closest to truth,' then the mechanism's equation of 'severity of tests passed' with 'high informational content' provides no account of why the *better-corroborated* (as opposed to merely more informative) theory should be preferred at all.","premise": "Popper's corroboration is a measure of the severity of tests a theory has already survived, which is logically distinct from a theory's empirical content (how much it rules out). A defense that operationalizes the preference solely as 'highest empirical content that has not yet been falsified' does not defend preference for the better-corroborated theory when two unfalsified theories share equal content but different test histories.","premise_evidence": [{"block": "5c99022a249cda440f49269f0b65d4c27b272947aa964bd9df8deb723d45b724", "quote": "The mechanism distinguishes between 'scientific rationality' (choosing the theory with highest empirical content that has not yet been falsified, to maximize learning potential) and 'pragmatic action' (where no scientific rationality applies). The preference is not based on the probability of truth (which Popper rejects) but on the severity of tests passed, which indicates high informational content."}],"counterexample": [{"theory_A": "High empirical content, unfalsified, has passed zero severe tests (newly conjectured yesterday).", "theory_B": "Identical empirical content, unfalsified, has passed decades of severe tests.", "observation": "Under the mechanism's criterion ('highest empirical content that has not yet been falsified'), A and B tie, so the mechanism provides no basis for preferring B—the better-corroborated theory—over A. Yet the claim asserts a defense of the preference for the *better-corroborated* theory."}],"successor_question": "If corroboration is reduced to 'empirical content + survival,' what distinguishes a newly proposed high-content theory from one that has survived decades of severe testing, and does the resulting rule defend anything stronger than Popper's already-uncontroversial preference for boldness?"}